@* CreateUser.razor — Phase 6.2 (Admin/Manager user creation)

   Staff-facing user-creation page at route `/Admin/Users/Create`. Reached
   from the "Create user" button on /Admin/Users (added in this same
   phase). Role-gated to Admin / Manager via [Authorize(Roles=...)] as
   defense-in-depth. The page-level gate is the first cut; the command
   handlers enforce stricter rules server-side:
     - CreateCustomerCommand: [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)]
     - CreateStaffCommand:    [RequireRoles(Admin, Manager)]  ← Managers
                                CAN create staff, but only Employee role

   PURPOSE:
     Lets admins/managers create new user accounts (staff or customer)
     via the UI, so test data can be seeded without DB access. Without
     this page, the only way to create users is via direct DB inserts
     or migrations — which blocks the full shopping-flow test (browse
     → cart → submit → /Sales) when no customer accounts exist.

   UNIFIED FORM (Customer + Staff):
     CreateCustomerCommand and CreateStaffCommand share 5 fields
     (WorkerId, FullName, Email, InitialPassword, Gender) and differ in:
       - Customer requires GroupId; Staff forbids it.
       - Customer always gets the "Customer" role; Staff requires an
         explicit Role selection (Employee / Manager / ReadOnly / Admin).
       - Customer creation: Manager or Admin. Staff creation: Admin or
         Manager (but Managers can only assign the Employee role).
     A single unified form with a User Type toggle avoids ~80% code
     duplication and lets an admin flip between types without navigating
     back. When a Manager is signed in, the Staff option is VISIBLE (they
     can create Employee staff), but the Staff Role dropdown is restricted
     to just Employee — this matches the server-side [RequireRoles(Admin,
     Manager)] on CreateStaffCommand + the Manager→Employee-only check in
     the handler (defense-in-depth).

   FORM FIELDS:
     1. User Type         (Customer / Staff toggle — visible to both
                           Admins and Managers; selecting Staff clears
                           GroupId, selecting Customer clears StaffRole.
                           Selected option gets a primary-color tinted
                           background + solid border; unselected is outlined.)
     2. Worker ID         (required, 1-100 chars — login identifier,
                           must be globally unique. AKA "Personnel Code"
                           in Persian — کد پرسنلی.)
     3. Full Name         (required, 1-200 chars)
     4. Initial Password  (required, 8-100 chars — show/hide toggle;
                           Identity's configured complexity rules are
                           the real authority. No default value.)
     5. Gender            (Male / Female card toggle — 2-value enum per
                           roadmap Section 12.5; NO DEFAULT — user must
                           pick explicitly to avoid creating users with
                           the wrong gender.)
     6. Group             (required when Customer; HIDDEN when Staff.
                           SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the field is now a
                           GroupId (Guid) selected from a 2-column grid of
                           existing-group cards (loaded from
                           GetAllCustomerGroupsQuery). The free-text "new
                           group name" mode is gone — admins must create
                           new groups via the CreateGroup page first,
                           then return here to assign the user.)
     7. Staff Role        (Employee / Manager / ReadOnly / Admin
                           dropdown — HIDDEN when Customer)

   EMAIL FIELD — REMOVED:
     Email was removed because employees don't have company emails and
     login uses WorkerId (UserName) — not email. The CreateCustomer /
     CreateStaff commands still accept an Email parameter for forward
     compatibility, but we pass empty string. Identity's
     RequireUniqueEmail=false (in appsettings.json) so empty emails
     don't collide. The validators were updated to make email optional.

   VALIDATION:
     - FluentValidation runs server-side via Wolverine's pipeline
       (CreateCustomerCommandValidator / CreateStaffCommandValidator).
       The validators produce the first wave of error messages
       (field-level, surfaced via EditContext validation).
     - The handlers do additional business-rule validation: WorkerId
       uniqueness (returns friendly "A user with worker ID 'X' already
       exists."), and Identity-level checks (duplicate email, weak
       password, role not seeded — surfaced via Result.Failure).
     - Client-side validation: minimal HTML5 required + min-length on
       password. The authoritative check is always server-side.

   ROLE-RESOLUTION PATTERN (CRITICAL):
     _isAdmin is computed via AuthStateProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync()
     in OnInitializedAsync — NOT via ICurrentUserService.IsInRole. The
     ICurrentUserService reads from IHttpContextAccessor.HttpContext,
     which is NULL over the SignalR circuit in interactive Blazor Server
     mode. AuthStateProvider reads from the Blazor circuit's persistent
     auth state, which IS preserved across circuit interactions.

     Same pattern as AdminUsers.razor (Phase 6.1), Sales.razor (4.2),
     and SaleDetail.razor (4.3). The Wolverine command handlers ALSO
     enforce [RequireRoles] server-side as defense-in-depth.

   FORM SUBMISSION:
     - On valid submit: dispatch the appropriate command
       (CreateCustomerCommand or CreateStaffCommand) via Wolverine.
     - On success: toast + navigate to /Admin/Users (where the new user
       appears in the list — sorted by created-at desc, so newest first).
     - On failure: show result.Error inline at the top of the form.
       Field-level errors from FluentValidation are surfaced via
       EditContext validation.

   LOCALIZATION:
     All user-visible strings via IStringLocalizer<CreateUser> (Loc).
     Keys defined in CreateUser.en-US.resx + CreateUser.fa-IR.resx.
     RTL is handled at the html dir level by MainLayout.

   ANTIFORGERY:
     Required — EditForm with OnValidSubmit. Blazor Server circuits
     already have an anti-forgery token established at circuit-start;
     EditForm validates it automatically. *@

@page "/Admin/Users/Create"
@* Policy: Policies.StaffManagement *@
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = "StaffManagement")]
@using System.Globalization
@using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Forms
@using Wolverine
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Authorization
@using TakOne.Application.Users.Commands.CreateCustomer
@using TakOne.Application.Users.Commands.CreateStaff
@using TakOne.Application.Customers.Queries.GetAllCustomerGroups
@using TakOne.Application.Customers.DTOs
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.Common
@using TakOne.WebUI.Services
@inject IMessageBus MessageBus
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject IStringLocalizer<CreateUser> Loc
@inject AuthenticationStateProvider AuthStateProvider
@inject ToastService Toast

<PageTitle>@Loc["PageTitle"]</PageTitle>

<HeadContent>
    <style>
            .create-grid {
                display: grid;
                grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
                gap: 2rem;
                align-items: start;
            }

            @@media (max-width: 992px) {
                .create-grid {
                    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
                    gap: 1.25rem;
                }
            }

            .form-section {
                display: flex;
                flex-direction: column;
                gap: 1.25rem;
            }

            .form-actions {
                display: flex;
                gap: 0.75rem;
                justify-content: flex-end;
                margin-top: 1rem;
            }

            .form-error {
                margin-bottom: 1rem;
            }

            /* Toggle option base — shared between user-type toggle, group-mode
                                       toggle, and group-list buttons. Selected state is denoted by the
                                       .is-selected class (primary-tinted bg + solid primary border). */
            .usertype-option {
                transition: background-color 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease;
            }

                .usertype-option.is-selected {
                    background-color: var(--rz-primary-lighter, rgba(var(--rz-primary-rgb, 67, 56, 202), 0.08));
                    border-color: var(--rz-primary) !important;
                }

            /* User-type + group-mode toggle — 2 cards side by side, wraps on narrow screens */
            .usertype-toggle {
                display: flex;
                gap: 0.5rem;
                flex-wrap: wrap;
            }

                .usertype-toggle .usertype-option {
                    flex: 1 1 200px;
                    min-width: 200px;
                }

            /* Group-list (existing groups) — 2-column grid of selectable cards.
                                       Each group name gets its own card; clicking selects it (single-
                                       select). Two cards per row keeps the list compact when there are
                                       many groups; long names wrap within their card. */
            .group-list {
                display: grid;
                grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
                gap: 0.5rem;
            }

            /* Group-section invalid state — applied to the outer container
                                       wrapping the entire group-name section (label + Existing/New
                                       toggle + group list) when the user has tried to
                                       submit without selecting a group. Mirrors the
                                       .gender-toggle.gender-invalid pattern: red border +
                                       danger-tinted background to draw the eye to the missing
                                       field. Toggled by the conditional class in the markup:
                                       _submitAttempted AND UserType is Customer AND GroupId is
                                       null/empty. The class falls away automatically on
                                       re-render as soon as the user picks an existing group
                                       (SelectExistingGroup sets GroupId). SALARY FEATURE
                                       (Step 8): the free-text New mode is gone — the form
                                       now binds to a Guid? GroupId via the group-card grid. */
            .group-section-invalid {
                border: 2px solid var(--rz-danger, #f87171);
                border-radius: var(--rz-border-radius-sm, 6px);
                padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
                background-color: rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.08);
                outline: none;
            }

            /* Inline error text below the group section (only rendered when the
                                       group is invalid). Color matches the border above. Same
                                       visual style as .gender-error-text. */
            .group-error-text {
                color: var(--rz-danger, #f87171);
                font-size: 0.8125rem;
                margin-top: 0.25rem;
                display: block;
            }

            /* Gender toggle — same visual pattern as the Customer/Staff toggle
                                       above (two RadzenCard buttons side-by-side, one selected at a
                                       time). Uses the same .usertype-option class so the selected
                                       state (primary-tinted bg + solid primary border) is identical.
                                       The .gender-toggle container is a flex row that wraps on narrow
                                       screens, matching .usertype-toggle's behavior. */
            .gender-toggle {
                display: flex;
                gap: 0.5rem;
                flex-wrap: wrap;
            }

                .gender-toggle .usertype-option {
                    flex: 1 1 200px;
                    min-width: 200px;
                }

                /* Gender-invalid state — applied to .gender-toggle when the user
                                       has tried to submit without picking a gender. The red border
                                       + danger-tinted background draws the eye to the toggle so the
                                       user understands what's missing. The EditForm's [Required]
                                       attribute on _model.Gender fires via DataAnnotationsValidator
                                       → OnInvalidSubmit → _submitAttempted = true, which is what
                                       toggles this class. The toggle cards themselves don't render
                                       a validation error natively — this CSS gives us a visible
                                       cue that matches the rest of the form's error style. */
                .gender-toggle.gender-invalid {
                    border: 2px solid var(--rz-danger, #f87171);
                    border-radius: var(--rz-border-radius-sm, 6px);
                    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
                    background-color: rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.08);
                    outline: none;
                }

            /* Inline error text under the toggle (only rendered when gender
                                       is invalid). Color matches the border above. */
            .gender-error-text {
                color: var(--rz-danger, #f87171);
                font-size: 0.8125rem;
                margin-top: 0.25rem;
                display: block;
            }

            .password-row {
                display: flex;
                gap: 0.5rem;
                align-items: flex-end;
            }

                .password-row .password-field {
                    flex: 1 1 auto;
                }

                .password-row .password-toggle {
                    flex: 0 0 auto;
                }

            /* Help panel on the right */
            .help-panel {
                position: sticky;
                top: 1rem;
            }

                .help-panel ul {
                    margin: 0.5rem 0 0 0;
                    padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
                    color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);
                    line-height: 1.7;
                }

                .help-panel li {
                    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
                }

            @@media (max-width: 992px) {
                .help-panel {
                    position: static;
                }
            }
    </style>
</HeadContent>

<RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="1rem">
    @* ── Breadcrumb + back button ──
       ButtonStyle.Secondary + Variant.Outlined (NOT ButtonStyle.Light) —
       the Light variant renders as flat gray text on light themes,
       which is invisible. Secondary gives a visible outlined button
       that works on both light and dark themes. *@
    <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.5rem">
        <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["BackToManageUsers"]"
                      Icon="arrow_back"
                      ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                      Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                      Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Admin/Users", forceLoad: false))" />
    </RadzenStack>

    @* ── Page header ── *@
    <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.75rem">
        <RadzenIcon Icon="person_add" Style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--rz-primary);" />
        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["PageHeading"]" TextStyle="TextStyle.H5" Style="font-weight: 600; margin: 0;" />
    </RadzenStack>

    @* ── Manager scope notice ──
       When a Manager (non-Admin) is signed in, they CAN create customer
       accounts AND staff accounts — but staff is restricted to the
       Employee role only (Manager/ReadOnly/Admin require Administrator
       access). We render this small informational banner above the form
       so the manager understands their scope. Admins don't see it. *@
    @if (_isManagerOnly)
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@Loc["ManagerScopeTitle"]"
                     Text="@Loc["ManagerScopeMessage"]"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Info"
                     Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                     ShowIcon="true" />
    }

    @* ── Main form ── *@
    <div class="create-grid">
        @* LEFT — form fields *@
        <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Style="padding: 1.5rem;">
            @* OnValidSubmit fires only when DataAnnotations validation passes.
               OnInvalidSubmit fires when validation fails (e.g. user submits
               without picking a gender — the [Required] on _model.Gender
               fires and prevents OnValidSubmit). We bind OnInvalidSubmit too
               so we can set _submitAttempted = true, which makes the gender
               toggle show its red-border + inline error state. Without
               this handler, the form would silently do nothing on submit
               when gender is missing — which was the bug the user reported. *@
            <EditForm Model="@_model"
                      OnValidSubmit="@HandleSubmitAsync"
                      OnInvalidSubmit="@HandleInvalidSubmit"
                      FormName="createUser">
                <DataAnnotationsValidator />

                @* Server-side error banner (e.g. "A user with worker ID 'X' already exists.") *@
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_submitError))
                {
                    <RadzenAlert Title="@Loc["ErrorTitle"]"
                                 Text="@_submitError"
                                 AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Danger"
                                 Variant="Variant.Filled"
                                 ShowIcon="true"
                                 Class="form-error" />
                }

                <div class="form-section">
                    @* ── User Type toggle ──
                       Both Admin AND Manager can pick Customer OR Staff.
                       (Phase 6.3: Managers used to be locked to Customer;
                       they can now create staff — but only with the
                       Employee role. See the Staff Role dropdown below
                       for the role restriction.) *@
                    <div>
                        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["UserTypeLabel"]"
                                    TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;" />
                        <div class="usertype-toggle">
                            <RadzenCard Variant="@(_model.UserType == UserType.Customer ? Variant.Filled : Variant.Outlined)"
                                        Class="@GetToggleClass(_model.UserType == UserType.Customer)"
                                        Style="@GetToggleBorderStyle(_model.UserType == UserType.Customer)"
                                        @onclick="@(() => OnUserTypeChanged(UserType.Customer))">
                                <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.5rem">
                                    <RadzenIcon Icon="shopping_bag" Style="@GetToggleIconStyle(_model.UserType == UserType.Customer)" />
                                    <div>
                                        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["UserTypeCustomer"]" TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle1" Style="@GetToggleTextStyle(_model.UserType == UserType.Customer)" />
                                        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["UserTypeCustomerHint"]"
                                                    TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); margin: 0;" />
                                    </div>
                                </RadzenStack>
                            </RadzenCard>

                            <RadzenCard Variant="@(_model.UserType == UserType.Staff ? Variant.Filled : Variant.Outlined)"
                                        Class="@GetToggleClass(_model.UserType == UserType.Staff)"
                                        Style="@GetToggleBorderStyle(_model.UserType == UserType.Staff)"
                                        @onclick="@(() => OnUserTypeChanged(UserType.Staff))">
                                <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.5rem">
                                    <RadzenIcon Icon="badge" Style="@GetToggleIconStyle(_model.UserType == UserType.Staff)" />
                                    <div>
                                        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["UserTypeStaff"]" TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle1" Style="@GetToggleTextStyle(_model.UserType == UserType.Staff)" />
                                        <RadzenText Text="@(_isManagerOnly ? Loc["UserTypeStaffHintManager"] : Loc["UserTypeStaffHint"])"
                                                    TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); margin: 0;" />
                                    </div>
                                </RadzenStack>
                            </RadzenCard>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    @* ── Worker ID ── *@
                    <div>
                        <RadzenFormField Text="@Loc["FieldWorkerId"]" Style="width: 100%;">
                            <RadzenTextBox @bind-Value="_model.WorkerId"
                                           Placeholder="@Loc["FieldWorkerIdPlaceholder"]"
                                           TValue="string"
                                           Style="width: 100%;" />
                        </RadzenFormField>
                        <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-top: 0.25rem;">
                            @Loc["WorkerIdHint"]
                        </RadzenText>
                    </div>

                    @* ── Full Name ── *@
                    <div>
                        <RadzenFormField Text="@Loc["FieldFullName"]" Style="width: 100%;">
                            <RadzenTextBox @bind-Value="_model.FullName"
                                           Placeholder="@Loc["FieldFullNamePlaceholder"]"
                                           TValue="string"
                                           Style="width: 100%;" />
                        </RadzenFormField>
                    </div>

                    @* ── Initial Password (with show/hide toggle) ── *@
                    <div>
                        <div class="password-row">
                            <RadzenFormField Text="@Loc["FieldPassword"]" Class="password-field" Style="width: 100%;">
                                @* v6.3: Switched from Value=/ValueChanged= (one-way binding
                                   with manual update) to @bind-Value (two-way binding).

                                   The previous Value="_model.InitialPassword" (WITHOUT the
                                   @ prefix) was a Razor STRING LITERAL, not a C# expression —
                                   the textbox displayed the literal text "_model.InitialPassword"
                                   instead of the model's value. Verified empirically via the
                                   compiled Razor source generator output:
                                     Value="x"        → AddComponentParameter(..., "x")     ← literal
                                     Value="@x"       → AddComponentParameter(..., x)       ← expression
                                     @bind-Value="x"  → Value + ValueChanged + ValueExpression

                                   @bind-Value also wires up ValueExpression, which is required
                                   for EditContext validation to fire. Without it, the [Required]
                                   attribute on InitialPassword was never checked client-side —
                                   the user could submit an empty password and see no validation
                                   error. With @bind-Value, the [Required] + [StringLength] rules
                                   now fire automatically, and RadzenFormField renders the
                                   validation message.

                                   The old comment claimed @bind-Value "can drop the current
                                   input" when toggling Type between text/password. This is not
                                   a real concern: RadzenTextBox syncs on every keystroke, and
                                   the value lives in _model — even if the DOM input element is
                                   re-rendered on Type change, the model retains the value and
                                   the next render restores it. All other RadzenTextBoxes on
                                   this page (WorkerId, FullName) already use
                                   @bind-Value with no issues. *@
                                <RadzenTextBox @bind-Value="_model.InitialPassword"
                                               Type="@(_showPassword ? "text" : "password")"
                                               Placeholder="@Loc["FieldPasswordPlaceholder"]"
                                               TValue="string"
                                               autocomplete="new-password"
                                               Style="width: 100%;" />
                            </RadzenFormField>
                            <RadzenButton Icon="@(_showPassword ? "visibility_off" : "visibility")"
                                          title="@(_showPassword ? Loc["PasswordHide"] : Loc["PasswordShow"])"
                                          aria-label="@(_showPassword ? Loc["PasswordHide"] : Loc["PasswordShow"])"
                                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Light"
                                          Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                          Click="@TogglePasswordVisibility" />
                        </div>
                        <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-top: 0.25rem;">
                            @Loc["PasswordHint"]
                        </RadzenText>
                    </div>

                    @* ── Gender ──
                       2-value enum per roadmap Section 12.5 (Male / Female).
                       Rendered as a 2-card toggle — same visual pattern as
                       the Customer/Staff toggle above. Both cards sit on
                       one line (flex row); the selected card gets the
                       primary-tinted background + solid primary border via
                       the .is-selected class. Only one can be selected at
                       a time because _model.Gender is a single nullable
                       enum — clicking the other card overwrites the value.

                       NO DEFAULT — the model's Gender is nullable (Gender?),
                       so neither card is pre-selected. The user MUST pick
                       one explicitly. This prevents creating users with the
                       wrong gender by accident. Submission is blocked client-
                       side via [Required] on the model (DataAnnotationsValidator
                       → OnInvalidSubmit → _submitAttempted = true) and server-
                       side via a manual null-check in HandleSubmitAsync.

                       VISIBLE ERROR STATE:
                       When the user clicks "Create user" without picking a
                       gender, the .gender-toggle container gets the
                       .gender-invalid CSS class (red border + danger-tinted
                       background around both cards) AND a localized inline
                       error message is shown below. The error clears as
                       soon as the user picks a gender (because
                       _model.Gender is no longer null, so the conditional
                       class falls away on re-render). *@
                    <div>
                        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["FieldGender"]"
                                    TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;" />
                        <div class="gender-toggle @(_submitAttempted && _model.Gender is null ? "gender-invalid" : "")">
                            <RadzenCard Variant="@(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Male ? Variant.Filled : Variant.Outlined)"
                                        Class="@GetToggleClass(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Male)"
                                        Style="@GetToggleBorderStyle(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Male)"
                                        @onclick="@(() => OnGenderChanged(Domain.Users.Gender.Male))">
                                <RadzenText Text="@Loc["GenderMale"]"
                                            TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle1"
                                            Style="@($"{GetToggleTextStyle(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Male)} text-align: center;")" />
                            </RadzenCard>
                            <RadzenCard Variant="@(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Female ? Variant.Filled : Variant.Outlined)"
                                        Class="@GetToggleClass(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Female)"
                                        Style="@GetToggleBorderStyle(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Female)"
                                        @onclick="@(() => OnGenderChanged(Domain.Users.Gender.Female))">
                                <RadzenText Text="@Loc["GenderFemale"]"
                                            TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle1"
                                            Style="@($"{GetToggleTextStyle(_model.Gender == Domain.Users.Gender.Female)} text-align: center;")" />
                            </RadzenCard>
                        </div>
                        @if (_submitAttempted && _model.Gender is null)
                        {
                            <span class="gender-error-text">@Loc["Error_GenderRequired"]</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                        Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-top: 0.25rem;">
                                @Loc["GenderHint"]
                            </RadzenText>
                        }
                    </div>

                    @* ── Group (Customer only) ──
                       Required for Customer (enforced server-side by the
                       validator and Domain invariant). Hidden for Staff —
                       staff users have null GroupId by design.

                       UX: 2-column grid of selectable cards (existing groups).
                       SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the Existing/New toggle is
                       gone — groups are now first-class aggregates
                       (CustomerGroup). The admin must create new groups
                       via the CreateGroup page first; this page only
                       assigns a user to an EXISTING group. If no groups
                       exist yet, the section shows an empty-state message
                       + a "Create new group" link button. Selecting a
                       group card sets _model.GroupId (Guid). *@
                    @if (_model.UserType == UserType.Customer)
                    {
                        var groupIsInvalid = _submitAttempted
                            && (_model.GroupId is null || _model.GroupId == Guid.Empty);
                        <div class="@(groupIsInvalid ? "group-section-invalid" : "")">
                            <RadzenText Text="@Loc["FieldGroupName"]"
                                        TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                        Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;" />

                            <div Style="margin-top: 0;">
                                @if (!_availableGroups.Any())
                                {
                                    <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Body2"
                                                Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                                        @Loc["NoExistingGroups"]
                                    </RadzenText>
                                    <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ActionCreateGroup"]"
                                                  Icon="group_add"
                                                  ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                                                  Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                                  Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                                                  Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Admin/Groups/Create", forceLoad: false))" />
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <div class="group-list">
                                        @foreach (var group in _availableGroups)
                                        {
                                            var groupId = group.Id;  // closure capture for the lambda
                                            var groupName = group.Name;  // display only
                                            var isSelected = _model.GroupId == groupId;
                                            <RadzenCard Variant="@(isSelected ? Variant.Filled : Variant.Outlined)"
                                                        Class="@GetToggleClass(isSelected)"
                                                        Style="@GetToggleBorderStyle(isSelected)"
                                                        @onclick="@(() => SelectExistingGroup(groupId))">
                                                <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center"
                                                             JustifyContent="JustifyContent.Center" Gap="0.5rem">
                                                    <RadzenIcon Icon="@(isSelected ? "check_circle" : "radio_button_unchecked")"
                                                                Style="@GetToggleIconStyle(isSelected)" />
                                                    <RadzenText Text="@groupName" TextStyle="TextStyle.Body1"
                                                                Style="@GetToggleTextStyle(isSelected)" />
                                                </RadzenStack>
                                            </RadzenCard>
                                        }
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                            <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                        Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                                @Loc["ExistingGroupHint"]
                            </RadzenText>

                            @* Inline error — shown when the user has tried to submit
                               without selecting a group. Mirrors the .gender-error-text
                               pattern: red text below the field, color-matched to the
                               .group-section-invalid border above. The error clears
                               automatically on re-render as soon as GroupId becomes
                               non-empty (via SelectExistingGroup). The top-of-form
                               _submitError banner also shows the same message, but this
                               inline error makes it impossible to miss — the user doesn't
                               need to scroll up to see what went wrong. *@
                            @if (groupIsInvalid)
                            {
                                <span class="group-error-text">@Loc["Error_GroupNameRequired"]</span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    }

                    @* ── Staff Role (Staff only) ──
                       Admin selects which staff role to assign at creation
                       (Employee / Manager / ReadOnly / Admin). The Customer
                       role is NOT in this list — staff accounts are never
                       customers at creation time. (A manager or employee
                       who wants to buy on their own behalf gets the Customer
                       role added later via AssignUserRoleCommand.)

                       MANAGER SCOPE (Phase 6.3):
                       Managers see the same dropdown but it only contains
                       "Employee" — Manager, ReadOnly, and Admin are excluded.
                       The Manager's _staffRoleOptions is built conditionally
                       in OnInitializedAsync. The CreateStaffCommandHandler
                       enforces the same rule server-side as defense-in-depth.

                       BINDING PATTERN:
                       We bind the dropdown to the PRIMITIVE role string
                       (TValue="string"), not to the complex StaffRoleOption
                       object. This matches the sibling pattern in
                       CreateProduct.razor (TValue="Guid?") and
                       AdminUsers.razor (TValue="ActivityFilter" enum).
                       RadzenDropDown's ValueProperty/TextProperty handle the
                       complex-to-primitive mapping; the binding itself
                       operates on the primitive key only. *@
                    @if (_model.UserType == UserType.Staff)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <RadzenFormField Text="@Loc["FieldStaffRole"]" Style="width: 100%;">
                                <RadzenDropDown @bind-Value="_model.StaffRole"
                                                Data="_staffRoleOptions"
                                                TValue="string"
                                                TextProperty="Label"
                                                ValueProperty="Value"
                                                Placeholder="@Loc["StaffRolePlaceholder"]"
                                                Style="width: 100%;" />
                            </RadzenFormField>
                            <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Caption"
                                        Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); display: block; margin-top: 0.25rem;">
                                @(_isManagerOnly ? Loc["StaffRoleHintManager"] : Loc["StaffRoleHint"])
                            </RadzenText>
                        </div>
                    }

                    @* ── Form actions ──
                       Cancel uses ButtonStyle.Secondary + Outlined (NOT Light)
                       — Light renders as flat gray on light themes, invisible. *@
                    <div class="form-actions">
                        <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["CancelButton"]"
                                      Icon="close"
                                      ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                                      Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                      Disabled="@_isSubmitting"
                                      Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Admin/Users", forceLoad: false))" />
                        <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["SubmitButton"]"
                                      Icon="save"
                                      ButtonType="ButtonType.Submit"
                                      ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                                      Variant="Variant.Filled"
                                      Disabled="@_isSubmitting" />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </EditForm>
        </RadzenCard>

        @* RIGHT — help panel (sticky on desktop) *@
        <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Class="help-panel" Style="padding: 1.25rem;">
            <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.5rem" Style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <RadzenIcon Icon="help_outline" Style="color: var(--rz-primary);" />
                <RadzenText Text="@Loc["HelpTitle"]" TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle1" Style="font-weight: 600; margin: 0;" />
            </RadzenStack>
            <ul>
                <li>@Loc["HelpWorkerIdUnique"]</li>
                <li>@Loc["HelpPassword"]</li>
                <li>@Loc["HelpGender"]</li>
                <li>@Loc["HelpGroupName"]</li>
                <li>@Loc["HelpStaffRole"]</li>
                <li>@Loc["HelpInitialPassword"]</li>
            </ul>
        </RadzenCard>
    </div>
</RadzenStack>

@code {
    // ── Form model ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// View-model for the create-user form. Holds fields for BOTH Customer
    /// and Staff creation in one flat object — the User Type toggle
    /// determines which fields are shown and which command is dispatched
    /// at submit time. Staff-only fields (StaffRole) and customer-only
    /// fields (GroupId) are simply ignored when the other type is
    /// selected.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed class CreateUserFormModel
    {
        // Validation attributes mirror the union of CreateStaffCommandValidator
        // and CreateCustomerCommandValidator rules. Fields used by BOTH
        // validators (WorkerId, FullName, InitialPassword, Gender) get
        // the shared rules. Fields used by only one (GroupId = customer-only,
        // StaffRole = staff-only) are validated conditionally in HandleSubmitAsync
        // (the existing manual checks at lines ~600-610 already do this and
        // produce localized Error_GroupNameRequired / Error_StaffRoleRequired
        // messages — we don't want to duplicate that logic with [Required]
        // because the attribute would fire even when the wrong UserType is
        // selected).
        //
        // The FluentValidation validators on the commands still run server-side
        // as defense-in-depth. Localized messages will be wired in Phase 7
        // item D (FluentValidation LanguageManager); for now, DataAnnotations'
        // default English messages are shown by <ValidationMessage> if added
        // to the markup.

        public UserType UserType { get; set; } = UserType.Customer;

        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Worker ID is required.")]
        [StringLength(100, ErrorMessage = "Worker ID cannot exceed 100 characters.")]
        public string WorkerId { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Full name is required.")]
        [StringLength(200, ErrorMessage = "Full name cannot exceed 200 characters.")]
        public string FullName { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        // Email field was REMOVED from the form. The commands still accept
        // an Email parameter (for forward compatibility), but we pass empty
        // string at submit time. The validators were updated to make email
        // optional. See file-header "EMAIL FIELD — REMOVED" for rationale.
        public string Email { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        // InitialPassword — no default value. The user MUST type something.
        // The placeholder is shown when empty, but the field itself starts blank.
        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Initial password is required.")]
        [StringLength(100, MinimumLength = 8, ErrorMessage = "Password must be between 8 and 100 characters.")]
        public string InitialPassword { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        // Gender — NULLABLE so the toggle has no default selection. The
        // user MUST pick Male or Female explicitly. This prevents creating
        // users with the wrong gender by accident. Submission is blocked
        // client-side via [Required] and server-side via a null-check in
        // HandleSubmitAsync (Error_GenderRequired).
        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Gender is required.")]
        public Domain.Users.Gender? Gender { get; set; } = null;

        // Customer-only — validated conditionally in HandleSubmitAsync
        // (Error_GroupNameRequired). No [Required] here because that would
        // fire even when UserType is Staff.
        // SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the field type changed from string
        // GroupName to Guid? GroupId. Groups are now first-class aggregates
        // (CustomerGroup) selected by Id, not by free-text name. The
        // "New group" inline-creation flow is gone — admins must create
        // groups via the CreateGroup page first.
        public Guid? GroupId { get; set; }

        // NOTE: the legacy GroupName + GroupMode fields were removed in
        // Step 8. The form now binds directly to GroupId (selected from
        // the GetAllCustomerGroupsQuery dropdown grid).

        /// <summary>
        /// The selected staff role's string value (e.g. "Employee",
        /// "Manager", "ReadOnly", "Admin"). Null when no role has been
        /// picked yet OR when UserType is Customer.
        ///
        /// We bind the PRIMITIVE string here (not the complex
        /// StaffRoleOption object) to match the sibling dropdown pattern.
        /// The Label is looked up from _staffRoleOptions at display time
        /// (e.g. for the success toast).
        ///
        /// Staff-only — validated conditionally in HandleSubmitAsync
        /// (Error_StaffRoleRequired). No [Required] here because that would
        /// fire even when UserType is Customer.
        /// </summary>
        public string? StaffRole { get; set; }
    }

    private enum UserType
    {
        Customer,
        Staff
    }

    // SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the GroupMode enum (Existing/New) was removed.
    // Groups are now first-class aggregates (CustomerGroup) selected by Id
    // from a single grid of existing-group cards — there is no "New" mode.
    // Admins create new groups via the CreateGroup page first.

    /// <summary>
    /// Strongly-typed staff-role option for the dropdown. The Value is the
    /// string role name as expected by CreateStaffCommand (matches the
    /// Roles constants). The Label is localized for display.
    ///
    /// BINDING NOTE:
    ///   The dropdown binds to the PRIMITIVE string Value (TValue="string"),
    ///   NOT to this complex object. The StaffRoleOption list is only used
    ///   as the Data source — RadzenDropDown's ValueProperty="Value" /
    ///   TextProperty="Label" handle the complex-to-primitive mapping.
    ///   This matches the sibling pattern in CreateProduct.razor (TValue="Guid?"
    ///   with CategoryDto data) and AdminUsers.razor (TValue="ActivityFilter"
    ///   with ActivityFilterOption data).
    /// </summary>
    private sealed class StaffRoleOption
    {
        public string Value { get; init; } = string.Empty;
        public string Label { get; init; } = string.Empty;
    }

    private CreateUserFormModel _model = new();

    // ── State ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    private bool _isAdmin;
    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the current user is in the Manager role AND NOT also in the
    /// Admin role. Used to:
    ///   - Show the manager-scope alert above the form.
    ///   - Restrict the staff-role dropdown to just Employee.
    ///   - Show the manager-specific staff-role hint under the dropdown.
    ///   - Pre-select Employee as the default StaffRole when a Manager
    ///     picks the Staff user-type (so they don't have to pick from a
    ///     one-item list — the value is already correct).
    /// Admins (who may also be in the Manager role) are treated as Admins
    /// (no restriction), so we explicitly check `!_isAdmin` here.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isManagerOnly;
    private bool _isSubmitting;
    private string _submitError = string.Empty;
    private bool _showPassword;

    /// <summary>
    /// Tracks whether the user has clicked the "Create user" submit button
    /// at least once. Used to gate the gender toggle's visible error
    /// state (red border + inline error message). The error clears as soon
    /// as the user picks a gender, because the markup re-renders on
    /// _model.Gender change and the conditional class falls away.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _submitAttempted;

    private List<StaffRoleOption> _staffRoleOptions = new();

    // Existing customer groups — loaded once from GetAllCustomerGroupsQuery
    // and rendered as a 2-column grid of selectable cards in the Group section
    // of the form. SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the type changed from List<string>
    // to List<CustomerGroupListItemDto> so each card carries both Id (for
    // selection) and Name (for display). The "New" mode is gone — admins must
    // create groups via the CreateGroup page first.
    private List<CustomerGroupListItemDto> _availableGroups = new();

    // ── Lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Resolve the current user's Admin/Manager roles via the Blazor auth state
        // (NOT ICurrentUserService.IsInRole — see file-header comment).
        try
        {
            var authState = await AuthStateProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();
            var user = authState.User;
            if (user?.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true)
            {
                _isAdmin = user.IsInRole(Roles.Admin);
                // Manager-only means "in Manager role AND not also Admin".
                // Admins who happen to also be in the Manager role are
                // treated as Admins (no restriction).
                _isManagerOnly = !_isAdmin && user.IsInRole(Roles.Manager);
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            // Non-fatal — _isAdmin stays false (Manager flow takes over as
            // the safer fallback), _isManagerOnly stays false too (no
            // manager-scope alert shown, full role list shown). This is
            // slightly worse UX but not a security issue — the server-side
            // [RequireRoles(Admin, Manager)] on CreateStaffCommand is the
            // real guard, and the handler enforces Manager→Employee-only.
            _isAdmin = false;
            _isManagerOnly = false;
        }

        // Populate the staff-role dropdown options. The Value strings
        // match the Roles constants exactly.
        //
        // MANAGER SCOPE (Phase 6.3): Managers see ONLY the Employee option.
        // Manager, ReadOnly, and Admin are excluded — the
        // CreateStaffCommandHandler rejects any non-Employee role from a
        // Manager as defense-in-depth. We also pre-select Employee as the
        // default StaffRole so the manager doesn't have to "pick" from a
        // one-item list — the value is already correct when they toggle
        // to Staff.
        if (_isManagerOnly)
        {
            _staffRoleOptions = new List<StaffRoleOption>
            {
                new() { Value = Roles.Employee, Label = Loc["RoleEmployee"] }
            };
            _model.StaffRole = Roles.Employee;  // pre-select
        }
        else
        {
            _staffRoleOptions = new List<StaffRoleOption>
            {
                new() { Value = Roles.Employee, Label = Loc["RoleEmployee"] },
                new() { Value = Roles.Manager,  Label = Loc["RoleManager"] },
                new() { Value = Roles.ReadOnly, Label = Loc["RoleReadOnly"] },
                new() { Value = Roles.Admin,    Label = Loc["RoleAdmin"] }
            };
        }

        // Load existing customer groups. Rendered as a 2-column grid of
        // selectable cards in the Group section of the form (Customer only).
        // Failure is non-fatal — the list will be empty and the Group section
        // shows a "no groups exist yet" message + a link to /Admin/Groups/Create.
        try
        {
            var groupsResult = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<List<CustomerGroupListItemDto>>>(
                new GetAllCustomerGroupsQuery { IncludeInactive = false });
            if (groupsResult.IsSuccess && groupsResult.Value is not null)
            {
                _availableGroups = groupsResult.Value
                    .OrderBy(g => g.Name, StringComparer.CurrentCulture)
                    .ToList();
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            // Non-fatal — leave _availableGroups as empty list.
            _availableGroups = new List<CustomerGroupListItemDto>();
        }

        // Note: Managers used to be locked to Customer here (Phase 6.2).
        // Phase 6.3 lifted that restriction — managers can now create both
        // customers AND staff (Employee only). The User Type toggle is
        // visible to both Admins and Managers; the Staff Role dropdown
        // restricts Managers to just Employee. No UserType lock here.
    }

    // ── Handlers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Toggles between Customer and Staff user types. Clears the
    /// type-specific fields (GroupId for Staff, StaffRole for Customer)
    /// so the form doesn't carry stale values across the toggle.
    /// </summary>
    private Task OnUserTypeChanged(UserType newType)
    {
        if (_model.UserType == newType) return Task.CompletedTask;

        _model.UserType = newType;

        if (newType == UserType.Staff)
        {
            // SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the legacy GroupName + GroupMode
            // fields are gone — just clear GroupId when switching to Staff
            // (staff users have no group).
            _model.GroupId = null;

            // Manager scope (Phase 6.3): when a Manager switches to Staff,
            // force StaffRole to Employee (they can't pick anything else
            // anyway — the dropdown only shows Employee — but this also
            // covers the case where they previously had a stale StaffRole
            // from a different state).
            if (_isManagerOnly)
            {
                _model.StaffRole = Roles.Employee;
            }
        }
        else
        {
            _model.StaffRole = null;
        }

        // Clear any stale submit error from a previous attempt.
        _submitError = string.Empty;
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): OnGroupModeChanged was removed — the
    // "Existing/New" toggle is gone. The Group section now shows only
    // a single grid of existing-group cards (no New mode).

    /// <summary>
    /// Sets the GroupId to the given existing-group Id. Called when
    /// the user clicks one of the group buttons in the Group section.
    /// Only one group can be selected at a time — clicking a different
    /// button overwrites GroupId with the new selection.
    /// </summary>
    private Task SelectExistingGroup(Guid groupId)
    {
        _model.GroupId = groupId;
        _submitError = string.Empty;  // clear stale error after a fresh pick
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Sets _model.Gender to the given value. Called when the user clicks
    /// one of the gender toggle cards. Only one gender can be selected at
    /// a time — clicking the other card overwrites the previous selection
    /// (the nullable enum enforces single-selection naturally, matching
    /// the radio-button semantics of the old InputRadioGroup but with the
    /// visual style of the Customer/Staff toggle cards above).
    ///
    /// Also clears any stale submit error from a previous attempt, so the
    /// red-border + inline error state disappears as soon as the user
    /// picks a gender (the markup's `_submitAttempted && _model.Gender
    /// is null` check also falls away on re-render).
    /// </summary>
    private Task OnGenderChanged(Domain.Users.Gender gender)
    {
        _model.Gender = gender;
        _submitError = string.Empty;  // clear stale error after a fresh pick
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Toggles the password visibility between masked and plaintext.
    /// Purely a UX affordance — no validation implications.
    /// </summary>
    private Task TogglePasswordVisibility()
    {
        _showPassword = !_showPassword;
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // Toggle helper methods — each takes a `bool selected` parameter so the
    // SAME helpers can be reused for:
    //   - The User Type toggle (Customer / Staff)  — pass `(_model.UserType == X)`
    //   - The Existing-group buttons list           — pass `(_model.GroupId == group.Id)`
    //
    // SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the Group Mode toggle (Existing / New) is gone
    // — only the User Type toggle and the Existing-group buttons list remain.
    //
    // WHY HELPERS (not inline ternaries):
    //   The original markup used `@{ var selected = ... }` + nested ternaries
    //   inside `@(...)` expressions, which confused the Razor parser (it
    //   started treating subsequent markup as code). Helper methods keep the
    //   markup clean and the parser happy.

    private string GetToggleClass(bool selected)
    {
        return selected ? "usertype-option is-selected" : "usertype-option";
    }

    private string GetToggleBorderStyle(bool selected)
    {
        var borderColor = selected ? "var(--rz-primary)" : "var(--rz-border-color)";
        return $"padding: 0.75rem; cursor: pointer; border: 2px solid {borderColor};";
    }

    private string GetToggleIconStyle(bool selected)
    {
        var color = selected ? "var(--rz-primary)" : "var(--rz-text-secondary-color)";
        return $"color: {color};";
    }

    private string GetToggleTextStyle(bool selected)
    {
        var color = selected ? "var(--rz-primary)" : "var(--rz-text-color)";
        return $"font-weight: 600; margin: 0; color: {color};";
    }

    // ── Submit ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Called by EditForm when DataAnnotations validation FAILS (e.g. user
    /// submitted without picking a gender — the [Required] on _model.Gender
    /// fires and prevents OnValidSubmit). We use this to set _submitAttempted
    /// so the gender toggle can show its red-border + inline error
    /// state. Without this handler, the form would silently do nothing on
    /// submit when gender is missing — which is exactly the bug the user
    /// reported in Phase 6.3 ("not choosing a gender and trying to create
    /// a customer or staff should show an error message and redden the
    /// radio buttons with an outline so users don't forget to set the
    /// gender").
    ///
    /// Note: we don't try to read EditContext here — _model is the source
    /// of truth, and the markup's `_submitAttempted && _model.Gender is null`
    /// check is enough to render the red border. Other [Required] failures
    /// (e.g. empty WorkerId) are surfaced by RadzenFormField's built-in
    /// validation message display.
    /// </summary>
    private Task HandleInvalidSubmit(EditContext editContext)
    {
        _submitAttempted = true;
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private async Task HandleSubmitAsync()
    {
        // Mark that a submit was attempted — used by the gender toggle
        // to show its red-border error state. We set this BEFORE any other
        // checks so the gender error is visible even if other fields also
        // fail validation.
        _submitAttempted = true;

        _submitError = string.Empty;

        // Defense-in-depth: Manager trying to create staff with a non-Employee
        // role. The dropdown only shows Employee when a Manager is signed in,
        // so this should never trigger in normal use — but if the request was
        // tampered with, the CreateStaffCommandHandler will reject it
        // server-side anyway. We surface a friendly message here too.
        if (_model.UserType == UserType.Staff && _isManagerOnly && _model.StaffRole != Roles.Employee)
        {
            _submitError = Loc["Error_ManagerCanOnlyCreateEmployee"];
            return;
        }

        // Staff requires a role selection. (Managers have Employee pre-selected,
        // so this only fires for Admins who left the dropdown on the placeholder.)
        if (_model.UserType == UserType.Staff && _model.StaffRole is null)
        {
            _submitError = Loc["Error_StaffRoleRequired"];
            return;
        }

        // Customer requires a non-empty GroupId. SALARY FEATURE (Step 8):
        // the field changed from string GroupName to Guid? GroupId — the
        // validation now checks for null/empty Guid instead of whitespace.
        if (_model.UserType == UserType.Customer
            && (_model.GroupId is null || _model.GroupId.Value == Guid.Empty))
        {
            _submitError = Loc["Error_GroupNameRequired"];
            return;
        }

        // Gender must be explicitly picked — the toggle has no default.
        if (_model.Gender is null)
        {
            _submitError = Loc["Error_GenderRequired"];
            return;
        }

        _isSubmitting = true;

        try
        {
            Result<Guid> result;

            if (_model.UserType == UserType.Customer)
            {
                // SALARY FEATURE (Step 8): the command takes GroupId (Guid)
                // instead of GroupName (string). The pre-check above already
                // ensured _model.GroupId is non-null and non-empty.
                var command = new CreateCustomerCommand(
                    WorkerId: _model.WorkerId.Trim(),
                    FullName: _model.FullName.Trim(),
                    GroupId: _model.GroupId!.Value,
                    Email: string.Empty,
                    InitialPassword: _model.InitialPassword,
                    Gender: _model.Gender.Value);

                result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<Guid>>(command);
            }
            else
            {
                var command = new CreateStaffCommand(
                    WorkerId: _model.WorkerId.Trim(),
                    FullName: _model.FullName.Trim(),
                    Email: string.Empty,
                    InitialPassword: _model.InitialPassword,
                    Role: _model.StaffRole!,
                    Gender: _model.Gender.Value);

                result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<Guid>>(command);
            }

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                // For the Staff success toast, look up the localized role
                // label (e.g. "Employee" → "کارمند") so the Persian toast
                // doesn't leak the Latin role constant.
                var staffRoleLabel = _model.StaffRole is null
                    ? string.Empty
                    : _staffRoleOptions.FirstOrDefault(o => o.Value == _model.StaffRole)?.Label ?? _model.StaffRole;

                var successMsg = _model.UserType == UserType.Customer
                    ? string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["CreateSuccessCustomer"], _model.FullName)
                    : string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["CreateSuccessStaff"], _model.FullName, staffRoleLabel);

                await Toast.Success(successMsg, summary: Loc["CreateSuccessTitle"]);

                // Navigate back to the admin user list — the new user
                // appears at the top (sorted by created-at desc, assuming
                // the list query orders that way; if not, the user can
                // search by worker ID).
                Navigation.NavigateTo("/Admin/Users", forceLoad: false);
            }
            else
            {
                // result.Error contains a user-friendly message from the
                // handler — e.g.:
                //   "A user with worker ID 'EMP-123' already exists."
                //   "کد پرسنلی «EMP-123» قبلاً ثبت شده است." (Identity, fa-IR)
                //   "ایمیل «foo@bar.com» قبلاً ثبت شده است." (Identity, fa-IR)
                //   "رمز عبور باید حداقل شامل یک کاراکتر غیر الفبایی-عددی باشد." (Identity, fa-IR)
                //
                // v6.2: Identity errors are now localized at the source via
                // TakOneIdentityErrorDescriber + IdentityErrorMessages.{culture}.resx,
                // so result.Error is in the request's UI culture (fa-IR or en-US)
                // — no per-call-site mapping required. The Code (e.g.
                // "PasswordRequiresNonAlphanumeric") is logged server-side by
                // UserAccountService / the handler, but no longer prefixed to
                // the user-facing string.
                _submitError = result.Error ?? Loc["Error_GenericCreate"];
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            _submitError = Loc["Error_GenericCreate"];
        }
        finally
        {
            _isSubmitting = false;
        }
    }
}